Normally, there would be a status line with the date/time and possibly
other relevant information (e.g. keyboard and IME status), and one or
more command shell windows (although you can close them and open them
again later, or configure to start something else).

A window can have "window indicators" for features of the program that is
running such as capabilities, e.g. a audio indicator for audio, and you
can use these indicators to control these things (e.g. audio volume and
audio redirection).

You might also be able to group windows together and have them into tabbed
groups, or to put subwindows arranged in whatever way you want to within
another window (which can itself be moved, hidden, etc), as well as to be
able to separate windows; this usually makes it unnecessary for application
programs to implement their own MDI and tabbed interface.

For the usual GUI widgets, and for the windows themselves and the window
indicators, they can be interoperated between other windows, including the
command shell. For example, you can use the data shown in one window with
other one that can process it (sort, filter, query, etc), or to drag a
command button from a GUI into the command shell to have it able to execute
as a part of a script (with conditions, loops, etc). (The other way around
can also be possible; you can create a GUI window with buttons and other
widgets to operate and display data with a program that does not have GUI.)

For text editing and text display, there would need to be a "reveal codes"
function (similar to WordPerfect), with the ability to disable ligatures,
kerns, and Composite Character VM, when the reveal codes is activated. The
reveal codes is necessary to make WYSIWYG editing in an acceptable way.
